  "description": {
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (en): Gossypium barbadense",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gossypium_barbadense",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 1378541477,
      "text": "Gossypium barbadense is one of several species of cotton. It is in the mallow family. It has been cultivated since antiquity, but has been especially prized since a form with particularly long fibers was developed in the 19th century. Other names associated with this species include Sea Island, Egyptian, Pima, and extra-long staple (ELS) cotton."
    },
    "es": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (es): Gossypium barbadense",
      "source_url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gossypium_barbadense",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 174423630,
      "text": "Gossypium barbadense es una de las especies de algodón más conocidas comercialmente, siendo nativa del oeste de Sudamérica, originalmente se domesticó en el área que comprende Perú y el sur de Ecuador, aunque su cultivo comercial se ha extendido a todo el mundo de tal forma que existen numerosas variedades desarrolladas. Pertenece al género Gossypium de la familia de las malváceas, es una planta neotropical perenne, con flores amarillas y semillas negras se caracteriza por sus fibras largas o extralargas en sus variedades comerciales. Es una de las siete especies conocidas tetraploides del género, todas de América o el Pacífico, que se originaron en un proceso de hibridación entre uno a dos millones de años atrás de dos especies diploides, entre las que se ha reconocido al silvestre Gossypium raimondii como uno de los posibles progenitores."
    },
    "fr": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Gossypium barbadense",
      "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gossypium_barbadense",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 239966810,
      "text": "Gossypium barbadense, le cotonnier créole, est une espèce d'arbuste de la famille des Malvaceae. Il atteint environ 1,5 m de haut, donnant un coton coloré, qui pousse en Amérique du Sud et dans les Antilles. Les fleurs sont jaunes ou blanches, les tiges rougeâtres. C'est une plante vivace ou annuelle, peu exigeante."
    },
    "pt": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (pt): Gossypium barbadense",
      "source_url": "https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gossypium_barbadense",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 70339352,
      "text": "O Gossypium barbadense é uma variedade de Algodão que produz fibras com comprimento extra-longo"
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Gossypium barbadense § Cultivation",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gossypium_barbadense#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
        "revision": 1378541477,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "The species accounts for about 5% of the world's cotton production.\nCertain regions specialize in G. barbadense. One reason is to prevent different species of cotton from hybridizing with each other. If a field of G. barbadense is too close to a field of a different species, the result is generally poor quality of the fiber.\nIn historical times, G. barbadense was mostly a dooryard crop and treated as a perennial. Individual households would maintain one or a few bushes for years at a time. This form of cultivation continues to a small extent to the present. However, nearly all cotton production, G. barbadense or otherwise, are now row crops and treated as an annual. Plants are grown from seed each year.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
